<office:document xmlns:office="urn:oasis:names:tc:opendocument:xmlns:office:1.0" xmlns:text="urn:oasis:names:tc:opendocument:xmlns:text:1.0" office:version="1.4" office:mimetype="application/vnd.oasis.opendocument.text" xmlns:style="urn:oasis:names:tc:opendocument:xmlns:style:1.0" xmlns:fo="urn:oasis:names:tc:opendocument:xmlns:xsl-fo-compatible:1.0" xmlns:draw="urn:oasis:names:tc:opendocument:xmlns:drawing:1.0" xmlns:svg="urn:oasis:names:tc:opendocument:xmlns:svg-compatible:1.0" xmlns:table="urn:oasis:names:tc:opendocument:xmlns:table:1.0">
 
 <office:automatic-styles>
  <text:list-style style:name="Lgrind1"><text:list-level-style-bullet text:level="1" text:bullet-char="•"><style:list-level-properties text:list-level-position-and-space-mode="label-alignment"><style:list-level-label-alignment text:label-followed-by="listtab" text:list-tab-stop-position="1.270cm" fo:text-indent="-0.635cm" fo:margin-left="1.270cm"/></style:list-level-properties></text:list-level-style-bullet><text:list-level-style-bullet text:level="2" text:bullet-char="◦"><style:list-level-properties text:list-level-position-and-space-mode="label-alignment"><style:list-level-label-alignment text:label-followed-by="listtab" text:list-tab-stop-position="1.905cm" fo:text-indent="-0.635cm" fo:margin-left="1.905cm"/></style:list-level-properties></text:list-level-style-bullet><text:list-level-style-bullet text:level="3" text:bullet-char="▪"><style:list-level-properties text:list-level-position-and-space-mode="label-alignment"><style:list-level-label-alignment text:label-followed-by="listtab" text:list-tab-stop-position="2.540cm" fo:text-indent="-0.635cm" fo:margin-left="2.540cm"/></style:list-level-properties></text:list-level-style-bullet><text:list-level-style-bullet text:level="4" text:bullet-char="•"><style:list-level-properties text:list-level-position-and-space-mode="label-alignment"><style:list-level-label-alignment text:label-followed-by="listtab" text:list-tab-stop-position="3.175cm" fo:text-indent="-0.635cm" fo:margin-left="3.175cm"/></style:list-level-properties></text:list-level-style-bullet><text:list-level-style-bullet text:level="5" text:bullet-char="◦"><style:list-level-properties text:list-level-position-and-space-mode="label-alignment"><style:list-level-label-alignment text:label-followed-by="listtab" text:list-tab-stop-position="3.810cm" fo:text-indent="-0.635cm" fo:margin-left="3.810cm"/></style:list-level-properties></text:list-level-style-bullet><text:list-level-style-bullet text:level="6" text:bullet-char="▪"><style:list-level-properties text:list-level-position-and-space-mode="label-alignment"><style:list-level-label-alignment text:label-followed-by="listtab" text:list-tab-stop-position="4.445cm" fo:text-indent="-0.635cm" fo:margin-left="4.445cm"/></style:list-level-properties></text:list-level-style-bullet><text:list-level-style-bullet text:level="7" text:bullet-char="•"><style:list-level-properties text:list-level-position-and-space-mode="label-alignment"><style:list-level-label-alignment text:label-followed-by="listtab" text:list-tab-stop-position="5.080cm" fo:text-indent="-0.635cm" fo:margin-left="5.080cm"/></style:list-level-properties></text:list-level-style-bullet><text:list-level-style-bullet text:level="8" text:bullet-char="◦"><style:list-level-properties text:list-level-position-and-space-mode="label-alignment"><style:list-level-label-alignment text:label-followed-by="listtab" text:list-tab-stop-position="5.715cm" fo:text-indent="-0.635cm" fo:margin-left="5.715cm"/></style:list-level-properties></text:list-level-style-bullet><text:list-level-style-bullet text:level="9" text:bullet-char="▪"><style:list-level-properties text:list-level-position-and-space-mode="label-alignment"><style:list-level-label-alignment text:label-followed-by="listtab" text:list-tab-stop-position="6.350cm" fo:text-indent="-0.635cm" fo:margin-left="6.350cm"/></style:list-level-properties></text:list-level-style-bullet><text:list-level-style-bullet text:level="10" text:bullet-char="•"><style:list-level-properties text:list-level-position-and-space-mode="label-alignment"><style:list-level-label-alignment text:label-followed-by="listtab" text:list-tab-stop-position="6.985cm" fo:text-indent="-0.635cm" fo:margin-left="6.985cm"/></style:list-level-properties></text:list-level-style-bullet></text:list-style>
  <style:style style:name="T1" style:family="text">
   <style:text-properties fo:font-weight="bold"/>
  </style:style>
  <style:style style:name="T2" style:family="text">
   <style:text-properties fo:font-family="monospace"/>
  </style:style>
  <style:style style:name="T3" style:family="text">
   <style:text-properties fo:font-style="italic"/>
  </style:style>
  <style:style style:name="T4" style:family="text">
   <style:text-properties fo:font-family="monospace" fo:font-weight="bold"/>
  </style:style>
 </office:automatic-styles><office:body>
  <office:text>
    <text:h text:outline-level="1">grind User Guide</text:h>
    <text:p>Draft of 16 October 2026. grind has no release yet. This guide describes the software as it is built today, and says so where a program lacks a feature or works differently.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="1">1. About this guide</text:h>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">1.1 Who this guide is for</text:h>
    <text:p>This guide is for people who write letters and reports, keep budgets and lists in spreadsheets, and want their files in an open format. You do not need to be a programmer. Chapters 1 to 7 cover everyday work in the windows, the terminal and the browser. Chapters 8 and 9 show how to repeat work from the command line and with scripts, and you can skip them until you need them.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">1.2 What grind is</text:h>
    <text:p>grind is an office suite with two kinds of document: <text:span text:style-name="T1">spreadsheets</text:span><text:s/>and <text:span text:style-name="T1">text documents</text:span>. Both are saved in the OpenDocument Format (ODF), the international standard that LibreOffice, Collabora Office and many other programs read and write. A document you make in grind is not locked to grind.</text:p>
    <text:p>The suite comes as several programs that share one engine. A formula, a spelling rule or a page layout means the same thing in all of them, and a document made in one opens in all the others.</text:p>
    <table:table table:name="Table1">
     <table:table-column table:number-columns-repeated="3"/>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">Program</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">What it is</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">Runs on</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>grind-sheet-gtk</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>The spreadsheet window</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Linux (GNOME)</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>grind-text-gtk</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>The word processor window</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Linux (GNOME)</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>grind-tui</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Both kinds of document, in a terminal</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Linux, macOS, Windows</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>grind web</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Both kinds of document, in a browser tab</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Any current browser</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>grind for Windows</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Both kinds of document, in one window</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Windows</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>grind for Mac</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Both kinds of document, in one window</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>macOS</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>grind</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>The command line, with every function</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Linux, macOS, Windows</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
    </table:table>
    <text:p>Which program you use is a matter of taste. Most screenshots in this guide show the Linux windows and the browser.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">1.3 What grind does differently</text:h>
    <text:p>Four things set grind apart from other office suites.</text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">You can see what is data and what is a calculation.</text:span><text:s/>A spreadsheet can mark every cell as an input, a formula or a label, and show the whole document as readable text. Section 3.12 explains how.</text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">Formulas can be read in plain English.</text:span><text:s/><text:span text:style-name="T2">=SUM(B2:B7)</text:span><text:s/>can be shown as <text:span text:style-name="T3">Sum(Number: B2:B7)</text:span>, and a long formula can be explained step by step.</text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">Everything works from the command line.</text:span><text:s/>Every function of every program is also a command, for people, for shell scripts and for other programs.</text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">Repetitive documents are built by scripts, not macros.</text:span><text:s/>grind has no macros, and never will. Instead, a small script can build a document from data, such as a letter for each person on a list.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">1.4 Conventions used in this guide</text:h>
    <text:p>Keys are written like <text:span text:style-name="T1">Ctrl+S</text:span>. A <text:span text:style-name="T1">bold</text:span><text:s/>word is something you see on the screen, such as a button or a menu entry. Text in <text:span text:style-name="T2">this typeface</text:span><text:s/>is something you type. A line that begins with <text:span text:style-name="T2">$</text:span><text:s/>is typed in a terminal, without the <text:span text:style-name="T2">$</text:span>.</text:p>
    <text:p>The keyboard shortcuts are those of the Linux windows. The Windows and Mac programs follow the conventions of their own platform. On a Mac, the <text:span text:style-name="T1">Command</text:span><text:s/>key takes the place of <text:span text:style-name="T1">Ctrl</text:span>.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="1">2. Getting started</text:h>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">2.1 Starting a program</text:h>
    <text:p>Start a program from your desktop&apos;s application menu, or from a terminal with the name of a file. Without a file name you start with an empty document.</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted Text">$ grind-sheet-gtk budget.fods</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted Text">$ grind-text-gtk letter.fodt</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted Text">$ grind-tui report.fodt</text:p>
    <text:p>The terminal program, <text:span text:style-name="T2">grind-tui</text:span>, reads the file to see whether it is a spreadsheet or a text document, so one command opens both. To start an empty text document in it, type <text:span text:style-name="T2">grind-tui --text</text:span>.</text:p>
    <text:p>The Windows and Mac programs, and the browser version, open on a welcome screen with three choices: a new spreadsheet, a new text document, or a document you already have.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">2.2 The spreadsheet window</text:h>
    <text:p><draw:frame text:anchor-type="paragraph"><draw:image draw:mime-type="image/png"><office:binary-data></office:binary-data></draw:image><svg:title>The spreadsheet window with a household budget</svg:title></draw:frame></text:p>
    <text:p>The window has four parts:</text:p>
    <text:list text:style-name="Lgrind1">
     <text:list-item>
      <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">The header bar</text:span><text:s/>at the top, with the document name, undo and redo, and buttons for opening files, searching, viewing options and the main menu.</text:p>
     </text:list-item>
     <text:list-item>
      <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">The format bar</text:span><text:s/>below it, which changes how the selected cells look.</text:p>
     </text:list-item>
     <text:list-item>
      <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">The formula bar</text:span>, which shows the address of the selected cell on the left and its content on the right.</text:p>
     </text:list-item>
     <text:list-item>
      <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">The grid</text:span>, with the sheet tabs below it.</text:p>
     </text:list-item>
    </text:list>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">2.3 The word processor window</text:h>
    <text:p><draw:frame text:anchor-type="paragraph"><draw:image draw:mime-type="image/png"><office:binary-data></office:binary-data></draw:image><svg:title>The word processor window with a short report</svg:title></draw:frame></text:p>
    <text:p>The word processor has a header bar like the spreadsheet&apos;s, a toolbar for block styles and character formatting, and the page you write on. The status bar at the bottom shows the kind of the current block, the number of words, the reading time and the spelling language. In the picture, the misspelt word <text:span text:style-name="T3">anual</text:span><text:s/>has a red wavy line.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">2.4 Opening documents</text:h>
    <text:p>Press <text:span text:style-name="T1">Ctrl+O</text:span><text:s/>to open a document. grind opens:</text:p>
    <text:list text:style-name="Lgrind1">
     <text:list-item>
      <text:p>OpenDocument spreadsheets and text documents (<text:span text:style-name="T2">.ods</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="T2">.fods</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="T2">.odt</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="T2">.fodt</text:span>),</text:p>
     </text:list-item>
     <text:list-item>
      <text:p>the plain-text <text:span text:style-name="T2">.grind</text:span><text:s/>form (section 9.4),</text:p>
     </text:list-item>
     <text:list-item>
      <text:p>Excel workbooks (<text:span text:style-name="T2">.xlsx</text:span>) and Word documents (<text:span text:style-name="T2">.docx</text:span>),</text:p>
     </text:list-item>
     <text:list-item>
      <text:p>CSV and TSV files, as spreadsheets,</text:p>
     </text:list-item>
     <text:list-item>
      <text:p>Markdown files (<text:span text:style-name="T2">.md</text:span>), as text documents.</text:p>
     </text:list-item>
    </text:list>
    <text:p>grind decides what a file is by reading it, not by its name. An Excel workbook, a Word document, a CSV or a Markdown file is converted into a <text:span text:style-name="T1">new, unsaved document</text:span>. The original file is never changed, and a message tells you what, if anything, could not be carried over. grind reads these formats but does not write Excel or Word files.</text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">Recent documents.</text:span><text:s/>The windows&apos; open dialog lists the documents you used last under <text:span text:style-name="T1">Recent</text:span>; on Windows they are also added to the system&apos;s recent documents. The terminal&apos;s welcome pane lists the last nine, and the keys <text:span text:style-name="T2">1</text:span><text:s/>to <text:span text:style-name="T2">9</text:span><text:s/>open them. The browser keeps a copy of the last four documents you opened or saved, because a web page cannot reopen a file from your disk; they are on its welcome pane and in the command palette, and <text:span text:style-name="T1">Forget the recent documents</text:span><text:s/>removes them.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">2.5 Saving documents</text:h>
    <text:p>Press <text:span text:style-name="T1">Ctrl+S</text:span><text:s/>to save. A document without a name asks for one. In the word processor, <text:span text:style-name="T1">Ctrl+Shift+S</text:span><text:s/>saves under a new name.</text:p>
    <text:p>grind writes ODF in two forms:</text:p>
    <text:list text:style-name="Lgrind1">
     <text:list-item>
      <text:p>A <text:span text:style-name="T1">flat</text:span><text:s/>file (<text:span text:style-name="T2">.fods</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="T2">.fodt</text:span>) is one readable text file. grind offers it first, because it works well with version control and because you can open it in a text editor if you ever need to.</text:p>
     </text:list-item>
     <text:list-item>
      <text:p>A <text:span text:style-name="T1">package</text:span><text:s/>(<text:span text:style-name="T2">.ods</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="T2">.odt</text:span>) is a compressed archive, which is what most other programs save by default.</text:p>
     </text:list-item>
    </text:list>
    <text:p>Both open in every program that reads ODF. Choose the form by the extension you give the file.</text:p>
    <text:p>When you save a document that another program made, grind keeps everything it does not understand itself, and writes it back unchanged. Saving a document never makes it worse: if grind could not write something back faithfully, it refuses to save rather than lose it.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">2.6 Undo and redo</text:h>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">Ctrl+Z</text:span><text:s/>undoes the last change and <text:span text:style-name="T1">Ctrl+Shift+Z</text:span><text:s/>redoes it. In the word processor, <text:span text:style-name="T1">Ctrl+Y</text:span><text:s/>also redoes. A change that touches many cells at once, such as a fill or a replace, is undone in one step.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="1">3. Working with spreadsheets</text:h>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">3.1 Cells and addresses</text:h>
    <text:p>The grid is made of numbered rows and lettered columns. The place where a row and a column meet is a <text:span text:style-name="T1">cell</text:span>, named by its column and row: the cell in column B, row 3 is <text:span text:style-name="T1">B3</text:span>. A rectangle of cells is a <text:span text:style-name="T1">range</text:span>, named by two corners: <text:span text:style-name="T1">B2:B7</text:span>. A cell on another sheet carries the sheet&apos;s name: <text:span text:style-name="T1">Prices.B2</text:span>.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">3.2 Moving around and selecting</text:h>
    <table:table table:name="Table2">
     <table:table-column table:number-columns-repeated="2"/>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">Keys</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">What they do</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Arrow keys</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Move one cell</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Ctrl+arrow</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Jump to the edge of the data</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Home, End</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Go to the start or end of the row</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Ctrl+Home, Ctrl+End</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Go to the start or end of the sheet</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Page Up, Page Down</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Move a screenful</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Shift with any of these</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Extend the selection</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Ctrl+A</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Select the whole sheet</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
    </table:table>
    <text:p>You can also click a cell, drag across a range, or click a row number or a column letter to select the whole row or column. To go to a cell, type its address, or the name of a named range, in the name box at the left of the formula bar.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">3.3 Entering data</text:h>
    <text:p>Select a cell and type. <text:span text:style-name="T1">Enter</text:span><text:s/>accepts the entry and moves down. <text:span text:style-name="T1">Tab</text:span><text:s/>accepts it and moves right. <text:span text:style-name="T1">Shift</text:span><text:s/>with either moves the other way. <text:span text:style-name="T1">Esc</text:span><text:s/>cancels. To change what is already in a cell, press <text:span text:style-name="T1">F2</text:span><text:s/>or double-click it.</text:p>
    <text:p>grind decides what you typed by one rule:</text:p>
    <text:list text:style-name="Lgrind1">
     <text:list-item>
      <text:p>A leading <text:span text:style-name="T2">=</text:span><text:s/>makes a formula.</text:p>
     </text:list-item>
     <text:list-item>
      <text:p>A number makes a number, read with the document&apos;s locale (section 3.10).</text:p>
     </text:list-item>
     <text:list-item>
      <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">TRUE</text:span><text:s/>and <text:span text:style-name="T2">FALSE</text:span><text:s/>make logical values.</text:p>
     </text:list-item>
     <text:list-item>
      <text:p>Anything else is text.</text:p>
     </text:list-item>
    </text:list>
    <text:p>To enter text that looks like a number or a formula, such as the part number <text:span text:style-name="T2">007</text:span>, start it with an apostrophe: <text:span text:style-name="T2">&apos;007</text:span>.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">3.4 Formulas</text:h>
    <text:p>A formula starts with <text:span text:style-name="T2">=</text:span>. It can use numbers, cell references, ranges, the operators <text:span text:style-name="T2">+ - * / ^</text:span>, the comparison operators <text:span text:style-name="T2">= &lt;&gt; &lt; &gt; &lt;= &gt;=</text:span>, and functions:</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted Text">=B2*1.19</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted Text">=SUM(B2:B7)</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted Text">=IF(C2&gt;B2; &quot;over budget&quot;; &quot;ok&quot;)</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted Text">=VLOOKUP(A2; Prices.A2:B40; 2; FALSE())</text:p>
    <text:p>Arguments are separated by a semicolon. When you press <text:span text:style-name="T1">Enter</text:span>, the cell shows the result and the formula bar keeps the formula.</text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">Help while typing.</text:span><text:s/>When you start a function name, grind lists the functions that fit. Press <text:span text:style-name="T1">Tab</text:span><text:s/>to accept one. A hint below the formula bar shows the function&apos;s arguments and which one you are typing. <text:span text:style-name="T1">Enter</text:span><text:s/>always finishes the cell, even while the list is shown.</text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">Pointing at cells.</text:span><text:s/>While you edit a formula, you can click a cell or use the arrow keys to put its address into the formula.</text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">Recalculation.</text:span><text:s/>A formula recalculates when a cell it depends on changes. Press <text:span text:style-name="T1">F9</text:span><text:s/>to recalculate the whole document.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">3.5 Functions</text:h>
    <text:p>grind implements the functions of the OpenFormula standard&apos;s core set, plus many common ones beyond it: 139 functions in all, among them <text:span text:style-name="T2">SUM</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="T2">AVERAGE</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="T2">COUNTIF</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="T2">SUMIF</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="T2">IF</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="T2">VLOOKUP</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="T2">ROUND</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="T2">DATE</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="T2">TODAY</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="T2">PMT</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="T2">TEXT</text:span><text:s/>functions such as <text:span text:style-name="T2">LEFT</text:span><text:s/>and <text:span text:style-name="T2">CONCATENATE</text:span>, and many more.</text:p>
    <text:p>To browse them, choose <text:span text:style-name="T1">Functions…</text:span><text:s/>from the main menu or the command palette. The list gives each function a plain-English name, a category and a short description. In a terminal, <text:span text:style-name="T2">grind sheet functions</text:span><text:s/>prints the same list.</text:p>
    <text:p>If a document uses a function that grind does not implement, the cell keeps the value it was saved with, and grind tells you which cells are affected.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">3.6 Reading a formula in plain English</text:h>
    <text:p>The formula bar can show a formula as a sentence. <text:span text:style-name="T2">=ROUND(A1; 2)</text:span><text:s/>reads as <text:span text:style-name="T3">Round(Value: A1, Digits: 2)</text:span>, and <text:span text:style-name="T2">=SUM(B2:B7)</text:span><text:s/>as <text:span text:style-name="T3">Sum(Number: B2:B7)</text:span>. This only changes how the formula is shown, never the formula itself. Turn it on and off with <text:span text:style-name="T1">Read Formulas as Sentences</text:span><text:s/>in the View menu.</text:p>
    <text:p>For a long, nested formula, choose <text:span text:style-name="T1">Explain This Formula</text:span>. It shows the formula taken apart, one function at a time, with the value of each part.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">3.7 Filling, copying and clearing</text:h>
    <text:p>To repeat a formula down a column, select the cell with the formula and the cells below it and press <text:span text:style-name="T1">Ctrl+D</text:span>. <text:span text:style-name="T1">Ctrl+R</text:span><text:s/>fills to the right. You can also drag the small square at the corner of the selection, the <text:span text:style-name="T1">fill handle</text:span>. References in the formula move along: <text:span text:style-name="T2">=B2*2</text:span><text:s/>filled down becomes <text:span text:style-name="T2">=B3*2</text:span>.</text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">Ctrl+C</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="T1">Ctrl+X</text:span><text:s/>and <text:span text:style-name="T1">Ctrl+V</text:span><text:s/>copy, cut and paste. A copied range is placed on the clipboard as tab-separated text, so it pastes into any other spreadsheet or a text editor. <text:span text:style-name="T1">Delete</text:span><text:s/>clears the selected cells.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">3.8 Formatting cells</text:h>
    <text:p>The format bar acts on the selected cells. From left to right it has bold, italic, underline and strikethrough; left, centre and right alignment; less and more indent; text wrapping; borders; text colour and fill colour; the number format; and <text:span text:style-name="T1">Clear</text:span>, which removes the formatting.</text:p>
    <text:p>The number format decides how a number is shown. Choose from the list:</text:p>
    <table:table table:name="Table3">
     <table:table-column table:number-columns-repeated="2"/>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">Format</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">Shows 1234.5 as</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>General</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>1234.5</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Number</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>1,234.50</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Percent</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>123450.00%</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Currency</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>1,234.50 €</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Date, Time, Date and time</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>the number as a date or a time</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Boolean</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>TRUE or FALSE</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Text</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>the value as it was typed</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
    </table:table>
    <text:p>The separators depend on the document&apos;s locale (section 3.10). Formatting never changes the value itself: a cell showing 1,235 may hold 1234.5, and calculations use 1234.5.</text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">Column widths and row heights.</text:span><text:s/>Drag the line between two column letters to change a column&apos;s width. Double-click it to fit the column to its widest entry. Rows work the same way. Rows grow by themselves to fit wrapped text.</text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">Zoom.</text:span><text:s/>Hold <text:span text:style-name="T1">Ctrl</text:span><text:s/>and turn the mouse wheel, or press <text:span text:style-name="T1">Ctrl++</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="T1">Ctrl+-</text:span><text:s/>and <text:span text:style-name="T1">Ctrl+0</text:span>.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">3.9 Sheets</text:h>
    <text:p>A document can have several sheets, shown as tabs at the bottom of the window. Click <text:span text:style-name="T1">+</text:span><text:s/>to add a sheet. Right-click a tab to rename or delete it. When you rename a sheet, grind updates every formula, name and chart that refers to it, and one <text:span text:style-name="T1">Ctrl+Z</text:span><text:s/>undoes the whole rename.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">3.10 The document&apos;s locale</text:h>
    <text:p>Every spreadsheet can state a <text:span text:style-name="T1">locale</text:span>, which decides how numbers are written and read. In a German document you type <text:span text:style-name="T2">1.234,50</text:span>; in an English one, <text:span text:style-name="T2">1,234.50</text:span>. Because the locale is stored in the document, everybody who opens it sees the same numbers. Set it in <text:span text:style-name="T1">Document Settings…</text:span><text:s/>in the main menu, which also shows a sample of how a number will look.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">3.11 Charts</text:h>
    <text:p>To draw a chart, select the table, including its headings, and choose <text:span text:style-name="T1">Insert a Chart</text:span><text:s/>from the command palette or the cell&apos;s context menu. The chart dialog shows a live preview. grind guesses which row or column holds the labels and whether the data runs down or across, and you can change the guess. Bar, line and pie charts are available.</text:p>
    <text:p>A chart sits on the sheet. Click it to select it, drag it to move it, and drag one of its eight handles to resize it. <text:span text:style-name="T1">Shift</text:span><text:s/>on a corner handle keeps the proportions. <text:span text:style-name="T1">Delete</text:span><text:s/>removes the selected chart, the arrow keys nudge it, and <text:span text:style-name="T1">Esc</text:span><text:s/>lets go.</text:p>
    <text:p>To change a chart after it is made, right-click it and choose <text:span text:style-name="T1">Edit Chart…</text:span>. The dialog changes the kind (bar, line or pie), the title, the legend, the cells it reads from, and the axes&apos; titles, labels and gridlines, with a preview as you go. Colours of a series or of a single bar or slice are set with <text:span text:style-name="T2">grind sheet chart-style</text:span>. In the browser the palette offers <text:span text:style-name="T1">Change the chart…</text:span>, and the Windows and Mac programs have a Chart menu that does the same in words.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">3.12 Seeing what a spreadsheet is made of</text:h>
    <text:p>A table of numbers does not show which numbers somebody typed and which a formula calculated. grind can show it.</text:p>
    <text:p><draw:frame text:anchor-type="paragraph"><draw:image draw:mime-type="image/png"><office:binary-data></office:binary-data></draw:image><svg:title>Cell roles: inputs in blue, calculations and labels marked</svg:title></draw:frame></text:p>
    <text:p>Turn on <text:span text:style-name="T1">Show What Each Cell Is</text:span><text:s/>in the View menu. Every cell gets a small mark:</text:p>
    <table:table table:name="Table4">
     <table:table-column table:number-columns-repeated="2"/>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">Mark</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">Meaning</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Filled diamond, blue</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>An input with a name, used by formulas</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Hollow diamond</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>An input, such as a number in a table</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>!</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>A number that a formula uses by its address, without a name. Consider naming it</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>=</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>A calculation on the same sheet</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Arrow</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>A calculation that reads another sheet</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>T</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>A label</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Cross</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>A formula that gives an error</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>~</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>A formula whose saved value is out of date</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
    </table:table>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">Show Where Names Live</text:span><text:s/>outlines every named range. <text:span text:style-name="T1">Show Formulas</text:span><text:s/>shows formulas instead of their results. <text:span text:style-name="T1">Show the Source</text:span><text:s/>(<text:span text:style-name="T1">Ctrl+Shift+U</text:span>) shows the whole document as plain text, one line per cell, and selecting a line selects its cell. None of these views changes the document.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">3.13 Finding and replacing</text:h>
    <text:p>Press <text:span text:style-name="T1">Ctrl+F</text:span><text:s/>to search. grind searches what the cells show, and moves to each match in turn. The replace field changes text and formulas in every matching cell, as one change that <text:span text:style-name="T1">Ctrl+Z</text:span><text:s/>undoes.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">3.14 Checking a spreadsheet</text:h>
    <text:p>Press <text:span text:style-name="T1">F8</text:span><text:s/>for <text:span text:style-name="T1">Check the Document</text:span>. grind lists problems such as a formula that gives an error, a reference to an empty cell, a saved value that is out of date, or a misspelt word. Click an entry to go to the cell.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">3.15 CSV files</text:h>
    <text:p>Opening a CSV or TSV file makes a new spreadsheet from it. To read a CSV file into the spreadsheet you have, use <text:span text:style-name="T1">Import CSV</text:span>; to write a range out, use <text:span text:style-name="T1">Export CSV</text:span>. grind finds the delimiter from the file&apos;s content. Fields that look like numbers become numbers, and a field such as <text:span text:style-name="T2">007</text:span><text:s/>stays text.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">3.16 Merging, hiding and turning cells</text:h>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">Merging.</text:span><text:s/>Select a range, right-click it and choose <text:span text:style-name="T1">Merge Cells</text:span>: its top-left cell is drawn across the whole range, for a title over a table. What the other cells held is kept out of sight and comes back with <text:span text:style-name="T1">Unmerge Cells</text:span>.</text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">Hiding rows and columns.</text:span><text:s/>Right-click a row number or a column letter and choose <text:span text:style-name="T1">Hide Row</text:span><text:s/>or <text:span text:style-name="T1">Hide Column</text:span>; with several selected, <text:span text:style-name="T1">Hide Rows</text:span><text:s/>or <text:span text:style-name="T1">Hide Columns</text:span>. A small marker shows where they are; click it to show them again. Calculations still use hidden cells.</text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">Row heights.</text:span><text:s/>A row grows by itself to hold wrapped or turned text. <text:span text:style-name="T1">Fit Row Height</text:span><text:s/>in the command palette gives rows back to their content after you changed them by hand.</text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">Turning text.</text:span><text:s/>The <text:span text:style-name="T1">Text Rotation</text:span><text:s/>button on the format bar turns the text of the selected cells: angled up or down, rotated to read upwards or downwards, or back to <text:span text:style-name="T1">No Rotation</text:span>. It is useful for narrow column headings.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">3.17 Checkboxes</text:h>
    <text:p>A cell can hold a <text:span text:style-name="T1">checkbox</text:span><text:s/>that shows whether another cell is TRUE or FALSE. Click it, or select its cell and press <text:span text:style-name="T1">Space</text:span>, to tick or untick it; the linked cell changes with it, so formulas can count what is ticked. Every program draws and ticks checkboxes. To add one, use the command line:</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted Text">$ grind sheet checkbox orders.fods E3 --link F3</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">3.18 Filters and tables</text:h>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">Filtering rows.</text:span><text:s/>Select a table, including its headings, and choose <text:span text:style-name="T1">Filter Rows</text:span><text:s/>from its context menu. Each heading gets a small button; click one and choose which values to show. Rows that do not match are hidden, not deleted, and calculations still use them. Choose <text:span text:style-name="T1">Filter Rows</text:span><text:s/>again to remove the filter.</text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">Formatting as a table.</text:span><text:s/><text:span text:style-name="T1">Format as Table…</text:span><text:s/>in the context menu does several things in one step: it adds the filter buttons, shades every other row, can add a <text:span text:style-name="T1">totals</text:span><text:s/>row under the table with a sum, average, count, minimum or maximum for each column, and gives the range a name such as <text:span text:style-name="T2">Table1</text:span>, which formulas can use. Everything it adds is ordinary formatting, so you can change any part of it afterwards. One <text:span text:style-name="T1">Ctrl+Z</text:span><text:s/>undoes all of it.</text:p>
    <text:p><draw:frame text:anchor-type="paragraph"><draw:image draw:mime-type="image/png"><office:binary-data></office:binary-data></draw:image><svg:title>A table with a merged title, filter buttons, banded rows, a totals row, a conditional-format rule and checkboxes</svg:title></draw:frame></text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">3.19 Conditional formatting</text:h>
    <text:p>A <text:span text:style-name="T1">rule</text:span><text:s/>draws cells differently while a formula is true: for example, every quantity over 10 in yellow, or a whole row in green once it is paid. Select the cells, right-click and choose <text:span text:style-name="T1">Conditional Formatting…</text:span>. Write the condition for the top-left cell, such as <text:span text:style-name="T2">=B3&gt;10</text:span>, and pick how the cells should look; grind moves the condition to every other cell the way filling moves a formula. A cell can have several rules, and the first one that holds is the one you see. The same dialog lists the sheet&apos;s rules and removes them.</text:p>
    <text:p>In the terminal the commands are <text:span text:style-name="T2">:rule yellow =B3&gt;10</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="T2">:rules</text:span><text:s/>and <text:span text:style-name="T2">:rule!</text:span>; in the browser the palette lists them under <text:span text:style-name="T1">Rules</text:span>.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="1">4. Working with text documents</text:h>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">4.1 Blocks</text:h>
    <text:p>A text document is a sequence of <text:span text:style-name="T1">blocks</text:span>. A block is a paragraph, a heading or a list item. <text:span text:style-name="T1">Enter</text:span><text:s/>ends a block and starts a new one, and <text:span text:style-name="T1">Backspace</text:span><text:s/>at the start of a block joins it to the one before.</text:p>
    <text:p>Choose a block&apos;s kind from the box at the left of the toolbar, or with the keyboard:</text:p>
    <table:table table:name="Table5">
     <table:table-column table:number-columns-repeated="2"/>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">Keys</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">Block kind</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Ctrl+0</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Paragraph</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Ctrl+1, Ctrl+2, Ctrl+3</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Heading 1, 2, 3</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Ctrl+L</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>List item</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
    </table:table>
    <text:p>The box also offers Title, Subtitle and Headings 4 to 6. Press <text:span text:style-name="T1">Tab</text:span><text:s/>at the start of a list item to indent it one level, and <text:span text:style-name="T1">Shift+Tab</text:span><text:s/>to bring it back.</text:p>
    <text:p>Headings make up the document&apos;s outline. <text:span text:style-name="T1">Ctrl+Shift+O</text:span><text:s/>opens it, and choosing a heading takes you there.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">4.2 Formatting text</text:h>
    <text:p>Select text and use the toolbar or the keys:</text:p>
    <table:table table:name="Table6">
     <table:table-column table:number-columns-repeated="2"/>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">Keys</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">Format</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Ctrl+B</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Bold</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Ctrl+I</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Italic</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Ctrl+U</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Underline</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
    </table:table>
    <text:p>The toolbar also has strikethrough, code, the font and the size, the text colour and the highlight colour, and <text:span text:style-name="T1">Clear</text:span>, which removes direct formatting. A button that looks pressed tells you the selection already has that format.</text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">Formatting as you type.</text:span><text:s/>You can type formatting the way many people do in plain text. When you type <text:span text:style-name="T2">**bold**</text:span>, the asterisks disappear and the word is bold. The same works for:</text:p>
    <table:table table:name="Table7">
     <table:table-column table:number-columns-repeated="2"/>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">You type</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">You get</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">**word**</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">bold</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">*word*</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T3">italic</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">__word__</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>underline</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">~~word~~</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>strikethrough</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">`word`</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>code</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2"># </text:span><text:s/>at the start of a line</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>a heading</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">- </text:span><text:s/>at the start of a line</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>a list item</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
    </table:table>
    <text:p>One <text:span text:style-name="T1">Ctrl+Z</text:span><text:s/>takes back the whole formatted word.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">4.3 Tables and pictures</text:h>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">Ctrl+Shift+T</text:span><text:s/>inserts a table. Click a cell to type in it; a cell can hold several paragraphs.</text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">Ctrl+Shift+I</text:span><text:s/>inserts a picture from a file. Give each picture <text:span text:style-name="T1">alternative text</text:span><text:s/>with <text:span text:style-name="T1">Ctrl+Alt+A</text:span>. Screen readers speak it, and it stands in for the picture where the picture cannot be shown.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">4.4 Going to a place</text:h>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">Ctrl+G</text:span><text:s/>goes to an address. <text:span text:style-name="T1">Ctrl+F</text:span><text:s/>searches. A place in a document can be named three ways:</text:p>
    <table:table table:name="Table8">
     <table:table-column table:number-columns-repeated="2"/>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">Address</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">Means</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">p12</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>The twelfth block</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">#intro</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>The block with the bookmark <text:span text:style-name="T3">intro</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">§2.1</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>The first subsection of section 2</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
    </table:table>
    <text:p>A bookmark or a section address keeps pointing at the same place when you edit above it. A block number does not.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">4.5 Pages, print preview and PDF</text:h>
    <text:p>While you write, a document is one continuous column. grind lays it out on pages when you print or export it.</text:p>
    <text:p><draw:frame text:anchor-type="paragraph"><draw:image draw:mime-type="image/png"><office:binary-data></office:binary-data></draw:image><svg:title>Print preview of a letter on A4</svg:title></draw:frame></text:p>
    <table:table table:name="Table9">
     <table:table-column table:number-columns-repeated="2"/>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">Keys</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">Command</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Ctrl+Shift+P</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Print preview</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Ctrl+Shift+E</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Export as PDF</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Ctrl+P</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Print</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
    </table:table>
    <text:p>The pages are those the document states: the paper size, the margins, and the header and footer with their page numbers. A document that states no page is printed on A4. grind prints only on the international ISO paper sizes, such as A4 and A5.</text:p>
    <text:p>The same document always produces the same PDF. The PDF has an outline of the headings, and it is tagged, so screen readers can read it.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">4.6 Headers, footers and letterheads</text:h>
    <text:p>A document&apos;s header and footer are printed on every page. A page number or a page count in one shows the right number on each page.</text:p>
    <text:p>A document can use different pages for different parts. A letter template, for example, can put a full letterhead on the first page and a short header on the pages after it, and draw fold marks at the edge of the paper. grind prints all of these as the template describes them. Section 9.2 shows how to fill such a template automatically.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">4.7 Markdown</text:h>
    <text:p>Markdown is a plain-text notation for headings, lists and emphasis. A <text:span text:style-name="T2">.md</text:span><text:s/>file opens as a new, unsaved document. <text:span text:style-name="T1">Import Markdown</text:span><text:s/>inserts a Markdown file into the document you have, and <text:span text:style-name="T1">Export Markdown</text:span><text:s/>(<text:span text:style-name="T1">Ctrl+Alt+S</text:span>) saves the document, or the selection, as a Markdown file. Pictures are written as files in a folder next to it.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">4.8 Word documents</text:h>
    <text:p>A Word document (<text:span text:style-name="T2">.docx</text:span>) opens as a new, unsaved text document. Headings, lists, tables, pictures, headers, footers and the page come across. A message names anything that does not. Save the result as an ODF document.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">4.9 Moving and deleting paragraphs</text:h>
    <text:p>Right-click a paragraph and choose <text:span text:style-name="T1">Move Paragraph Up</text:span><text:s/>or <text:span text:style-name="T1">Move Paragraph Down</text:span><text:s/>to swap it with its neighbour, or <text:span text:style-name="T1">Delete Paragraph</text:span><text:s/>to remove it. With several paragraphs selected, they move or go together. Each is one step for <text:span text:style-name="T1">Ctrl+Z</text:span>. A heading moved this way leaves its section behind; to move a whole section, use <text:span text:style-name="T2">grind text move</text:span><text:s/>(section 8.3).</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="1">5. Spelling and grammar</text:h>
    <text:p>grind checks spelling as you type, in text documents and in the text of spreadsheet cells. A word grind does not know gets a red wavy line. The word you are still typing is left alone until you finish it.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">5.1 Languages</text:h>
    <text:p>grind checks English (US), German, French, Spanish, Italian, Portuguese (Portugal) and Polish.</text:p>
    <text:p>Unless you choose otherwise, spelling is checked in <text:span text:style-name="T1">All languages</text:span>: a word is right if any of the seven dictionaries knows it, so a French name in an English letter is not marked. Suggestions come from the document&apos;s main language: the one the document states, or else the one most of its words are in.</text:p>
    <text:p>To check in one language alone, or to turn spelling off, use the <text:span text:style-name="T1">language picker</text:span><text:s/>at the right-hand end of the status bar. It shows a globe for all languages, the flag of the language&apos;s country for one, or <text:span text:style-name="T1">Off</text:span>; click it to choose. On Windows and in the terminal it shows letters instead, <text:span text:style-name="T2">ALL</text:span><text:s/>or <text:span text:style-name="T2">DE</text:span>. The same choice is in the menus:</text:p>
    <text:list text:style-name="Lgrind1">
     <text:list-item>
      <text:p>In the spreadsheet window, the View menu (the eye button) has <text:span text:style-name="T1">Spelling Language</text:span>, next to <text:span text:style-name="T1">Check Spelling</text:span>, which turns spelling on and off (<text:span text:style-name="T1">Shift+F7</text:span>).</text:p>
     </text:list-item>
     <text:list-item>
      <text:p>In the word processor window, it is the <text:span text:style-name="T1">Spelling</text:span><text:s/>submenu of the main menu.</text:p>
     </text:list-item>
     <text:list-item>
      <text:p>In the browser, the command palette lists <text:span text:style-name="T1">Spelling: all languages</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="T1">Spelling: off</text:span><text:s/>and one entry for each language.</text:p>
     </text:list-item>
     <text:list-item>
      <text:p>In the terminal, type <text:span text:style-name="T2">:spell de</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="T2">:spell auto</text:span><text:s/>(all languages) or <text:span text:style-name="T2">:spell off</text:span>.</text:p>
     </text:list-item>
    </text:list>
    <text:p>Your choice is remembered, separately for spreadsheets and for text documents, and it holds for every document you open until you change it. It is your setting, and it is never written into a document. If you turn spelling off, it stays off.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">5.2 Correcting a word</text:h>
    <text:p>Right-click a misspelt word. The menu lists suggestions; choose one to replace the word. <text:span text:style-name="T1">Ignore All</text:span><text:s/>accepts the word until you close the program. <text:span text:style-name="T1">Add to Dictionary</text:span><text:s/>keeps it in your own word list for good.</text:p>
    <text:p><draw:frame text:anchor-type="paragraph"><draw:image draw:mime-type="image/png"><office:binary-data></office:binary-data></draw:image><svg:title>The command palette in the browser, listing the spelling commands</svg:title></draw:frame></text:p>
    <text:p>In the browser, put the cursor in the word and press <text:span text:style-name="T1">Ctrl+K</text:span>: the palette lists the suggestions, <text:span text:style-name="T1">Ignore</text:span><text:s/>and <text:span text:style-name="T1">Add to your dictionary</text:span>, and <text:span text:style-name="T1">Go to the next misspelling</text:span>. The browser fetches a language&apos;s dictionary the first time it needs it, so the first check in a language takes a moment.</text:p>
    <text:p>In a spreadsheet only typed text is checked: never a number, a date or the result of a formula.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">5.3 Your personal dictionary</text:h>
    <text:p>Words you keep with <text:span text:style-name="T1">Add to Dictionary</text:span><text:s/>go into your <text:span text:style-name="T1">personal dictionary</text:span>. It holds for every document and every language, and it is never written into a document. To see it, remove a word or add one by hand, open <text:span text:style-name="T1">Personal Dictionary…</text:span>: in the word processor it is in the <text:span text:style-name="T1">Spelling</text:span><text:s/>submenu, in the spreadsheet window in the View menu and under the language picker. In the browser the palette has <text:span text:style-name="T1">Spelling: your dictionary…</text:span><text:s/>and <text:span text:style-name="T1">Spelling: add a word to your dictionary…</text:span>. In the terminal, <text:span text:style-name="T2">:dict</text:span><text:s/>lists the words, and <text:span text:style-name="T2">:dict add word</text:span><text:s/>and <text:span text:style-name="T2">:dict rm word</text:span><text:s/>change the list. From the command line:</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted Text">$ grind words</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted Text">$ grind words --add Hunspell</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted Text">$ grind words --remove Hunspell</text:p>
    <text:p>The windows, the terminal and the command line share one list, kept in a file of yours (<text:span text:style-name="T2">~/.config/grind/words</text:span>). The browser keeps its own.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">5.4 Grammar</text:h>
    <text:p>grind also checks grammar in text documents. A built-in checker looks for common mistakes in English and German: a word written twice, <text:span text:style-name="T3">its</text:span><text:s/>where <text:span text:style-name="T3">it&apos;s</text:span><text:s/>is meant, <text:span text:style-name="T3">then</text:span><text:s/>for <text:span text:style-name="T3">than</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="T3">a</text:span><text:s/>before a vowel sound, a missing comma before <text:span text:style-name="T3">dass</text:span><text:s/>or <text:span text:style-name="T3">weil</text:span>, a space before a full stop, and more. Each paragraph is checked in the language its own words are in. The checker is on unless you turn it off, and nothing leaves your computer.</text:p>
    <text:p>A finding gets a blue wavy line, and a suggestion about style a dotted one (the Mac marks them with green dots). Right-click it: the menu starts with what is wrong and what it could be instead, and choosing one replaces the text. <text:span text:style-name="T1">Ignore Rule</text:span><text:s/>stops that kind of finding from being reported, in every document, until you report it again (section 10).</text:p>
    <text:p><draw:frame text:anchor-type="paragraph"><draw:image draw:mime-type="image/png"><office:binary-data></office:binary-data></draw:image><svg:title>The word processor window, with grammar findings underlined in an English and a German paragraph</svg:title></draw:frame></text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">Style suggestions</text:span><text:s/>point out wordiness rather than mistakes: <text:span text:style-name="T3">in order to</text:span><text:s/>where <text:span text:style-name="T3">to</text:span><text:s/>will do, <text:span text:style-name="T3">at this point in time</text:span>, too many <text:span text:style-name="T3">very</text:span>s, a sentence of more than 40 words. They are off unless you turn them on.</text:p>
    <text:list text:style-name="Lgrind1">
     <text:list-item>
      <text:p>In the word processor window, the main menu has a <text:span text:style-name="T1">Grammar</text:span><text:s/>submenu with <text:span text:style-name="T1">Check Grammar</text:span>, which turns grammar on and off, and <text:span text:style-name="T1">Style Suggestions</text:span>.</text:p>
     </text:list-item>
     <text:list-item>
      <text:p>On Windows, the View menu has <text:span text:style-name="T1">Check Grammar</text:span><text:s/>and <text:span text:style-name="T1">Grammar Style Suggestions</text:span>, and <text:span text:style-name="T1">Ctrl+F7</text:span><text:s/>goes to the next finding.</text:p>
     </text:list-item>
     <text:list-item>
      <text:p>On the Mac, the Spelling menu has <text:span text:style-name="T1">Check Grammar With Spelling</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="T1">Grammar Style Suggestions</text:span><text:s/>and <text:span text:style-name="T1">Next Grammar Finding</text:span><text:s/>(<text:span text:style-name="T1">Option+Cmd+;</text:span>).</text:p>
     </text:list-item>
     <text:list-item>
      <text:p>In the browser, the palette has <text:span text:style-name="T1">Grammar: check</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="T1">Grammar: off</text:span><text:s/>and <text:span text:style-name="T1">Grammar: style suggestions too</text:span>. With the cursor in a finding, <text:span text:style-name="T1">Ctrl+K</text:span><text:s/>lists its replacements and <text:span text:style-name="T1">Ignore this rule</text:span>.</text:p>
     </text:list-item>
     <text:list-item>
      <text:p>In the terminal, <text:span text:style-name="T2">}</text:span><text:s/>and <text:span text:style-name="T2">{</text:span><text:s/>go to the next and previous finding and show what is wrong on the status line; <text:span text:style-name="T2">:fix 1</text:span><text:s/>takes the first replacement. <text:span text:style-name="T2">:grammar off</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="T2">:grammar on</text:span><text:s/>and <text:span text:style-name="T2">:grammar style on</text:span><text:s/>are the switches.</text:p>
     </text:list-item>
    </text:list>
    <text:p>Your choices are remembered for every document.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">5.5 LanguageTool</text:h>
    <text:p>The built-in checker is small on purpose. For a much fuller check, in many more languages, grind can ask <text:span text:style-name="T1">LanguageTool</text:span>, a free grammar checker that runs as a server. You can run it on your own computer, use LanguageTool&apos;s public server, or use a LanguageTool Premium account. Until you set one up, grind does not ask it.</text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">On your own computer.</text:span><text:s/>With Podman or Docker installed, start a server:</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted Text">$ podman run -d --rm --name grind-lt -p 8010:8010 docker.io/erikvl87/languagetool</text:p>
    <text:p>The image is made by the community, not by LanguageTool; any LanguageTool server will do. Then tell grind where it is, and check that it answers:</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted Text">$ grind grammar http://localhost:8010 --with-builtin</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted Text">$ grind grammar --check-server</text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">--with-builtin</text:span><text:s/>keeps the built-in checker on as well. Its findings appear at once and LanguageTool&apos;s a moment later; where both mark the same words, LanguageTool&apos;s is the one shown. Your text stays on your computer.</text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">LanguageTool&apos;s public server.</text:span><text:s/><text:span text:style-name="T2">grind grammar public</text:span><text:s/>needs no setup, but your text is sent to LanguageTool over the internet, and the server takes only a limited amount of text a minute. On the command line, a whole document is sent there only with <text:span text:style-name="T2">--public-ok</text:span>.</text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">LanguageTool Premium.</text:span><text:s/>Put your account&apos;s user name and API key in the environment, then choose it:</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted Text">$ export GRIND_LANGUAGETOOL_USER=you@example.com</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted Text">$ export GRIND_LANGUAGETOOL_KEY=your-api-key</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted Text">$ grind grammar premium</text:p>
    <text:p>grind never writes the key to a file.</text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">grind grammar builtin</text:span><text:s/>goes back to the built-in checker, and <text:span text:style-name="T2">grind grammar off</text:span><text:s/>turns grammar off altogether. <text:span text:style-name="T2">grind grammar</text:span><text:s/>on its own prints what is set. The setting is yours, kept in <text:span text:style-name="T2">~/.config/grind/grammar</text:span>, and the windows and the terminal follow it. They ask LanguageTool shortly after you stop typing, only about the paragraphs that changed, and say where your text goes. If the server does not answer, grind says so once and tries again later. In the terminal, <text:span text:style-name="T2">:grammar http://localhost:8010</text:span><text:s/>or <text:span text:style-name="T2">:grammar public</text:span><text:s/>sets it as well.</text:p>
    <text:p>The browser has a setting of its own: the palette&apos;s <text:span text:style-name="T1">Grammar: LanguageTool server…</text:span><text:s/>asks for an address or <text:span text:style-name="T2">public</text:span>, and <text:span text:style-name="T1">Grammar: LanguageTool off</text:span><text:s/>stops asking. A server on your computer must allow the page to ask it (LanguageTool&apos;s <text:span text:style-name="T2">--allow-origin</text:span><text:s/>option), which the container above does. Premium is not offered in the browser, because the page keeps no keys.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="1">6. Using grind in a terminal</text:h>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">grind-tui</text:span><text:s/>runs in any terminal. It works like the editor vi: the keyboard is in one of a few <text:span text:style-name="T1">modes</text:span>.</text:p>
    <table:table table:name="Table10">
     <table:table-column table:number-columns-repeated="3"/>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">Mode</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">For</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">Enter it with</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Normal</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Moving around</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">Esc</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Insert</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Typing</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">i</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Visual</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Selecting</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">v</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Command</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Commands</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">:</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
    </table:table>
    <text:p>The title bar at the top shows the document and its sheets or headings. The status bar at the bottom shows the mode, your position and whether there are unsaved changes.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">6.1 Normal and Visual mode</text:h>
    <table:table table:name="Table11">
     <table:table-column table:number-columns-repeated="2"/>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">Key</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">What it does</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">h</text:span><text:s/><text:span text:style-name="T2">j</text:span><text:s/><text:span text:style-name="T2">k</text:span><text:s/><text:span text:style-name="T2">l</text:span><text:s/>or the arrows</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Move</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">i</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Start typing</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">v</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Start selecting</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">y</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Copy the selection</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">p</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Paste</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">d</text:span><text:s/>or <text:span text:style-name="T2">x</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Delete</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">u</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Undo</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">n</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="T2">N</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Next and previous search match</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">]</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="T2">[</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Next and previous misspelt word</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">*</text:span><text:s/><text:span text:style-name="T2">/</text:span><text:s/><text:span text:style-name="T2">_</text:span><text:s/><text:span text:style-name="T2">~</text:span><text:s/>(Visual mode)</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Bold, italic, underline, strikethrough</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
    </table:table>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">6.2 Commands</text:h>
    <text:p>Type <text:span text:style-name="T2">:</text:span><text:s/>and a command, then <text:span text:style-name="T1">Enter</text:span>.</text:p>
    <table:table table:name="Table12">
     <table:table-column table:number-columns-repeated="2"/>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">Command</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">What it does</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">:w</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Save</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">:q</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Quit; <text:span text:style-name="T2">:q!</text:span><text:s/>quits without saving</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">:wq</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Save and quit</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">:help</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Show help</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">:find word</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Search</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">:s/old/new/</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Replace (spreadsheet)</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">:recalc</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Recalculate (spreadsheet)</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">:roles</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Mark what each cell is (spreadsheet)</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">:outline</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>List the headings (text)</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">:table</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Insert a table (text)</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">:mark name</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Set a bookmark (text)</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">:pdf</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Export as PDF (text)</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">:spell</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Show the spelling language; <text:span text:style-name="T2">:spell de</text:span><text:s/>chooses one</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">:fix 1</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Replace a misspelt word with the first suggestion</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">:lint</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Check the document</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">:source</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Show the document as plain text</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
    </table:table>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">6.3 More commands</text:h>
    <table:table table:name="Table15">
     <table:table-column table:number-columns-repeated="2"/>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">Command or key</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">What it does</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">}</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="T2">{</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Next and previous grammar finding (text)</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">:grammar off</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Grammar off; <text:span text:style-name="T2">:grammar on</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="T2">:grammar style on</text:span>, and <text:span text:style-name="T2">:grammar ignore</text:span><text:s/>for the rule at the cursor (text)</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">:grammar http://localhost:8010</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Ask LanguageTool as well (section 5.5)</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">:dict</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Your personal dictionary; <text:span text:style-name="T2">:dict add word</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="T2">:dict rm word</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">:move p12</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Move this block before p12 (text)</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">:print</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Print the document, through the system&apos;s <text:span text:style-name="T2">lp</text:span><text:s/>(text)</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">:merge</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="T2">:unmerge</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Merge the selected cells, or undo the merge</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">:hide</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="T2">:show</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Hide or show the selected columns; <text:span text:style-name="T2">:hide rows</text:span><text:s/>and <text:span text:style-name="T2">:show rows</text:span><text:s/>for rows</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">:rotate up</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Turn the text: <text:span text:style-name="T2">45</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="T2">up</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="T2">down</text:span><text:s/>or <text:span text:style-name="T2">none</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">:filter</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Filter buttons over the table; again to remove them</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">:format-table --totals</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Format the table, with a totals row</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">:rule yellow =B3&gt;10</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>A conditional format; <text:span text:style-name="T2">:rules</text:span><text:s/>lists them, <text:span text:style-name="T2">:rule!</text:span><text:s/>removes them</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Space</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Tick or untick a checkbox</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">:welcome</text:span></text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell>
       <text:p>Back to the welcome pane</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
    </table:table>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">y</text:span><text:s/>copies to the system clipboard as well, in terminals that allow it, so you can paste into other programs. <text:span text:style-name="T2">:help</text:span><text:s/>lists every command.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="1">7. Using grind in the browser</text:h>
    <text:p><draw:frame text:anchor-type="paragraph"><draw:image draw:mime-type="image/png"><office:binary-data></office:binary-data></draw:image><svg:title>A spreadsheet in the browser</svg:title></draw:frame></text:p>
    <text:p>The browser version is a web page with no menus. It has one bar with <text:span text:style-name="T1">Open…</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="T1">Save</text:span>, undo and redo, and <text:span text:style-name="T1">Commands</text:span>, which opens the command palette. A second bar holds the formatting tools for the kind of document that is open. A spreadsheet adds the formula bar and the sheet tabs.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">7.1 The command palette</text:h>
    <text:p>Press <text:span text:style-name="T1">Ctrl+K</text:span>, or click <text:span text:style-name="T1">Commands</text:span>, type a few letters of what you want, and press <text:span text:style-name="T1">Enter</text:span>. The palette holds every command that has no button. It is also the way to go somewhere: type an address such as <text:span text:style-name="T2">B12</text:span><text:s/>or <text:span text:style-name="T2">p12</text:span>, the name of a sheet, a named range, a heading or a bookmark.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">7.2 Opening and saving</text:h>
    <text:p>Click <text:span text:style-name="T1">Open…</text:span>, or drop a file anywhere on the page. <text:span text:style-name="T1">Save</text:span><text:s/>downloads a copy to your downloads folder. It cannot write over the file you opened, because a web page has no access to your files.</text:p>
    <text:p>Your documents never leave your computer: the page reads and writes them itself. Your spelling choices and your own word list are kept by the browser for this page only, and clearing the page&apos;s data clears them.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">7.3 Shortcuts and recent documents</text:h>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">F1</text:span><text:s/>lists the page&apos;s keyboard shortcuts. What the windows do from menus is in the palette: for example <text:span text:style-name="T1">Merge cells</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="T1">Hide row(s)</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="T1">Rotate text up</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="T1">Filter rows</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="T1">Format as table</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="T1">Format as table with totals…</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="T1">Conditional format for the selection…</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="T1">Change the chart…</text:span><text:s/>and the <text:span text:style-name="T1">Grammar</text:span><text:s/>commands (section 5.4).</text:p>
    <text:p>The page keeps a copy of the last four documents you opened or saved, up to about 750 KB each, in the browser&apos;s storage. The welcome pane lists them, and in the palette they are <text:span text:style-name="T1">Open</text:span><text:s/>and the document&apos;s name. <text:span text:style-name="T1">Forget the recent documents</text:span><text:s/>removes them.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="1">8. The command line</text:h>
    <text:p>Every function of grind is also a command. This is useful for repeating a job, for working on a server, and for checking a file without opening it.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">8.1 The shape of a command</text:h>
    <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted Text">$ grind &lt;area&gt; &lt;verb&gt; &lt;file&gt; [arguments]</text:p>
    <text:p>The area is <text:span text:style-name="T2">sheet</text:span><text:s/>for spreadsheets and <text:span text:style-name="T2">text</text:span><text:s/>for text documents. A few verbs work on any document and find out what it is by reading it: <text:span text:style-name="T2">info</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="T2">lint</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="T2">convert</text:span><text:s/>and <text:span text:style-name="T2">project</text:span>.</text:p>
    <text:p>Each command reads the file, does one thing and writes the file back. Two options work with every command: <text:span text:style-name="T2">--dry-run</text:span><text:s/>shows what a command would do without writing anything, and <text:span text:style-name="T2">--format json</text:span><text:s/>gives the answer in a form other programs can read. Add <text:span text:style-name="T2">--help</text:span><text:s/>to any command for a full description.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">8.2 A spreadsheet from the command line</text:h>
    <text:p>This example makes a small spreadsheet, adds a formula and shows the result:</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted Text">$ grind sheet new book.ods</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted Text">$ grind sheet set book.ods A1 1</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted Text">$ grind sheet set book.ods A2 &apos;=[.A1]*2&apos;</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted Text">$ grind sheet recalc book.ods</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted Text">$ grind sheet view book.ods A1:A2</text:p>
    <text:p>On the command line, formulas are written in ODF&apos;s own form, as they are stored in the file: <text:span text:style-name="T2">[.A1]</text:span><text:s/>is the cell A1. The <text:span text:style-name="T2">eval</text:span><text:s/>command tries out a formula without storing it. Give it the cell the formula would sit in, because relative references depend on that; <text:span text:style-name="T2">--trace</text:span><text:s/>shows the value of every part:</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted Text">$ grind sheet eval book.ods A3 &apos;=SUM([.A1:.A2])&apos;</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted Text">$ grind sheet eval book.ods A3 &apos;=SUM([.A1:.A2])&apos; --trace</text:p>
    <text:p>Other useful commands:</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted Text">$ grind sheet import-csv book.ods data.csv</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted Text">$ grind sheet export-csv book.ods --delimiter tab</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted Text">$ grind sheet format book.ods B2:B9 currency</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted Text">$ grind sheet locale book.ods de-DE</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted Text">$ grind sheet functions</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">8.3 A text document from the command line</text:h>
    <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted Text">$ grind text new letter.fodt</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted Text">$ grind text import-md letter.fodt notes.md</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted Text">$ grind text view letter.fodt</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted Text">$ grind text export-md letter.fodt</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted Text">$ grind text export-pdf letter.fodt letter.pdf --paper a4</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted Text">$ grind text preview letter.fodt page1.png --page 1</text:p>
    <text:p>A block is addressed as in the window: <text:span text:style-name="T2">p12</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="T2">#intro</text:span><text:s/>or <text:span text:style-name="T2">§2.1</text:span>.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">8.4 Any document</text:h>
    <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted Text">$ grind info report.fodt</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted Text">$ grind lint report.fodt</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted Text">$ grind convert book.fods book.ods</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted Text">$ grind convert book.fods book.grind</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted Text">$ grind sheet import workbook.xlsx workbook.fods</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted Text">$ grind text import letter.docx letter.fodt</text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">grind info</text:span><text:s/>tells you what a document is and what is in it. <text:span text:style-name="T2">grind lint</text:span><text:s/>checks it and ends with an error when it finds one, so you can use it in a script. <text:span text:style-name="T2">grind convert</text:span><text:s/>changes between the package, the flat file and the <text:span text:style-name="T2">.grind</text:span><text:s/>form. The two <text:span text:style-name="T2">import</text:span><text:s/>commands convert an Excel workbook or a Word document into ODF.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">8.5 Spelling from the command line</text:h>
    <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted Text">$ grind text spell report.fodt --suggest</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted Text">$ grind text spelling de</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted Text">$ grind sheet spelling off</text:p>
    <text:p>The first command lists the misspelt words in a document, with suggestions. <text:span text:style-name="T2">grind text spelling</text:span><text:s/>and <text:span text:style-name="T2">grind sheet spelling</text:span><text:s/>set the remembered language for each kind of document, the same setting the windows use. Without a value they print the current one.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">8.6 Grammar from the command line</text:h>
    <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted Text">$ grind text grammar letter.fodt</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted Text">p2+75 <text:s text:c="2"/>builtin:repeated-word <text:s text:c="2"/>grammar <text:s text:c="2"/>the <text:s text:c="2"/>“the” twice</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted Text">p3+8 <text:s text:c="3"/>builtin:its-it&apos;s <text:s text:c="7"/>grammar <text:s text:c="2"/>its <text:s text:c="2"/>“it&apos;s”: it is <text:s text:c="2"/>it&apos;s</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted Text">$ grind text correct letter.fodt p3+8 its &quot;it&apos;s&quot;</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted Text">$ grind text grammar letter.fodt --picky</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted Text">$ grind text grammar letter.fodt --languagetool http://localhost:8010</text:p>
    <text:p>Each line is where a finding starts, its rule, its kind, the text it covers, what is wrong and what it could be instead. <text:span text:style-name="T2">grind text correct</text:span><text:s/>makes one change, and refuses if the text there is no longer what you named. <text:span text:style-name="T2">--picky</text:span><text:s/>adds style suggestions. <text:span text:style-name="T2">--languagetool</text:span><text:s/>asks a server this once, whatever is set; <text:span text:style-name="T2">--builtin</text:span><text:s/>uses the built-in checker alone. <text:span text:style-name="T2">grind lint</text:span><text:s/>reports the built-in checker&apos;s findings too, so a script can stop on them. Section 5.5 shows how <text:span text:style-name="T2">grind grammar</text:span><text:s/>sets the checker, and <text:span text:style-name="T2">grind grammar --ignore</text:span><text:s/>with a rule&apos;s name stops reporting it.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">8.7 More commands</text:h>
    <text:p>Every command has <text:span text:style-name="T2">--help</text:span>. Beyond those above, these are worth knowing:</text:p>
