    </table:table>
    <text:h text:outline-level="1">9. Automating work</text:h>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">9.1 Scripts that build documents</text:h>
    <text:p>A script is a small text file that describes a document. It is written in a simple language called Rhai. A script cannot read your files, use the network or start programs. It can read one data file in JSON format from its own folder. Because of these limits, the same script and data always produce exactly the same document.</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted Text">$ grind build budget.rhai -o budget.fods</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted Text">$ grind test budget.rhai</text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">grind build</text:span><text:s/>builds the document. <text:span text:style-name="T2">grind test</text:span><text:s/>runs the checks written into the script, such as &quot;the total is the sum of the rows&quot;, and reports any that fail.</text:p>
    <text:p>Scripts suit documents you make again and again from changing data: a monthly report, a loan schedule, a grade book, or a letter to each person on a list. The <text:span text:style-name="T2">examples</text:span><text:s/>folder has a working script for each of these.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">9.2 A letter to each person on a list</text:h>
    <text:p>A script can return several documents at once. grind then writes them into a folder, one file each. The letter examples write one business letter per recipient, laid out on a DIN 5008 template, as PDF files:</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted Text">$ grind build examples/letters/nebenkosten.rhai -o letters --as pdf</text:p>
    <text:p>The template is an ordinary text document with the page, the letterhead and the paragraph styles. The script fills in the recipient, the date and the figures, and calculates the figures itself. You design the template in any word processor and save it as a flat text document (<text:span text:style-name="T2">.fodt</text:span>).</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">9.3 Checking documents automatically</text:h>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">grind lint</text:span><text:s/>looks for the mistakes people make in spreadsheets and documents: a formula that gives an error, a saved value that is out of date, a style that does not exist, a misspelt word. It never changes the document, and it ends with an error when it finds one, so it can check documents automatically.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">9.4 The plain-text form</text:h>
    <text:p>The <text:span text:style-name="T2">.grind</text:span><text:s/>form writes a document as readable text. A spreadsheet becomes a list of sheets, rows and cells:</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted Text">$ grind sheet project book.ods</text:p>
    <text:p>You can edit the <text:span text:style-name="T2">.grind</text:span><text:s/>file in any text editor and open it again in grind. A change to one cell changes one line of the file, which makes the form useful for reviewing a spreadsheet somebody else made and for keeping documents in a version-control system such as Git.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">9.5 Writing scripts in an editor</text:h>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">grind init</text:span><text:s/>sets up a directory for writing scripts and plain-text documents in an editor: a starter script with its data and tests, and settings, snippets and recommended extensions for VS Code. In a git repository it also lets <text:span text:style-name="T2">git diff</text:span><text:s/>show the cells that changed in a spreadsheet. It never overwrites a file you changed.</text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T2">grind lsp</text:span><text:s/>is a language server: point your editor&apos;s language client at it, and it shows problems on the right line, a formula&apos;s result beside it, and completion for functions and names. <text:span text:style-name="T2">grind definitions</text:span><text:s/>prints what an editor needs to complete a script&apos;s functions.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="1">10. Troubleshooting</text:h>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">A file does not open.</text:span><text:s/>Check what the file is with <text:span text:style-name="T2">grind info</text:span><text:s/>followed by the file name. Password-protected files cannot be opened. A flat file is plain text, so a damaged one can be read in a text editor.</text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">A cell shows an error such as </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T4">#DIV/0!</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T1"><text:s/>or </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T4">#NAME?</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T1">.</text:span><text:s/>Select the cell and read its formula, or let grind explain it (section 3.6). <text:span text:style-name="T1">Check the Document</text:span><text:s/>(<text:span text:style-name="T1">F8</text:span>) lists every cell with an error.</text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">A cell shows </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T4">###</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T1">.</text:span><text:s/>The column is too narrow for the number. Widen it, or double-click the line at the right of the column letter.</text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">Numbers look wrong.</text:span><text:s/>Check the document&apos;s locale (section 3.10). <text:span text:style-name="T2">1.234</text:span><text:s/>is one thousand two hundred thirty-four in German and one and a bit in English.</text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">Spelling underlines almost every word.</text:span><text:s/>The document is being checked in one language that is not its own. Choose the right one, or <text:span text:style-name="T1">All languages</text:span><text:s/>(section 5.1).</text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">Grammar marks something that is right.</text:span><text:s/>Right-click it and choose <text:span text:style-name="T1">Ignore Rule</text:span>. To have the rule reported again, run <text:span text:style-name="T2">grind grammar</text:span><text:s/>to see the rules you ignored, and <text:span text:style-name="T2">grind grammar --unignore</text:span><text:s/>with the rule&apos;s name. Style suggestions can be turned off on their own (section 5.4).</text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">LanguageTool finds nothing.</text:span><text:s/>Run <text:span text:style-name="T2">grind grammar --check-server</text:span>: it says whether the server answers, and which version it is. Check that the container is running (<text:span text:style-name="T2">podman ps</text:span>). In the browser, the server must be started with <text:span text:style-name="T2">--allow-origin</text:span>.</text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">A document looks different in another program.</text:span><text:s/>Programs can draw the same ODF slightly differently, and a font that is not installed is replaced. To share exactly how a page looks, export a PDF.</text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">A command fails.</text:span><text:s/>Add <text:span text:style-name="T2">--help</text:span><text:s/>to the command to see what it expects. <text:span text:style-name="T2">grind --help</text:span><text:s/>lists all commands.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="1">Appendix A: Keyboard shortcuts</text:h>
    <text:p>These are the shortcuts of the Linux windows. In the word processor, <text:span text:style-name="T1">Ctrl+?</text:span><text:s/>shows the full list. In the spreadsheet window, anything without a shortcut is in the command palette (<text:span text:style-name="T1">Ctrl+K</text:span>); in the word processor, it is in the main menu.</text:p>
